[description]

[b]Azure Pipelines[/b] defines CI/CD in YAML checked into the repository—closer to Oh My Git’s workflow levels where automation reacts to pushes, but here the orchestrator lives in Azure DevOps. A run clones your repo (full or shallow), executes jobs on agents, publishes results back to PRs as build validation, and optionally deploys through gated environments.

[b]YAML structure:[/b] [b]stages[/b] group dependent work; [b]jobs[/b] target agent pools; [b]steps[/b] run tasks (bash, PowerShell, marketplace). Use [code]extends[/code] and templates to deduplicate microservice pipelines; pin template refs to tags or SHAs so org-wide edits cannot surprise every repo at once.

[b]Triggers and PR validation:[/b] [code]trigger[/code] and [code]pr:[/code] blocks decide which branch events enqueue runs—align them with branch policies requiring specific pipeline success on PRs. Missing [code]pr:[/code] triggers is a frequent reason PR gates stay red while CI on [code]main[/code] is green.

[b]Agents:[/b] Microsoft-hosted agents are convenient but generic images; self-hosted pools reach private networks, GPUs, and compliance enclaves at the cost of patching and trust. Agents authenticate with registration keys or PAT-like tokens—rotate on staff departure. Treat self-hosted agents as sensitive: they execute arbitrary PR code from forks unless restricted.

[b]Environments and deployments:[/b] environments wrap deployment targets with approvals, branch filters, and checks (REST gates, delays). Map human release ceremony onto YAML: staging auto-deploys on [code]main[/code]; production waits for approver plus green smoke tests.

[b]Service connections and artifacts:[/b] service connections store cloud credentials—scope narrowly to subscription plus resource group. Pipeline runs publish packages, symbols, and test results to Artifacts feeds; set retention policies to control storage cost.

[b]Classic vs YAML pipelines:[/b] older classic UI-defined pipelines still exist in long-lived orgs—migrating to [code]azure-pipelines.yml[/code] in Git keeps pipeline changes reviewable in PRs alongside application code. Mixed models confuse branch policy build validation pointing at the wrong definition ID; inventory both types during audits.

[b]OIDC and federated credentials:[/b] prefer workload identity federation over long-lived secrets in YAML where supported—tokens are short-lived and scoped to the pipeline run, reducing blast radius if a log leak occurs during fork experimentation.

[b]When things go wrong:[/b] queued jobs that never start usually mean agent pool capacity zero or demands mismatch ([code]demands: docker[/code] on a pool without Docker). Fork PRs exfiltrating secrets happen when secret variables are available to builds from forks—disable or use manual approval for external contributions. “Template not found” after refactor means a broken [code]@main[/code] pin—use version tags.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] treat agents like cattle in the cloud but pets on-prem: both need health checks, capacity planning, and secret hygiene.
